WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.090
This is exclusive video of Elon giving a lecture
at his home in Bel-Air.

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:07.390
Is everybody listening?

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:12.374
He didn't think regular schools were doing
a good enough job of preparing kids.

00:00:18.606 --> 00:00:23.575
He’s a big proponent of teaching people
to think based on First Principles, which

00:00:23.575 --> 00:00:28.515
is figuring out how things work by boiling
them down to the basics.

00:00:28.515 --> 00:00:34.746
For example, it used to be that people thought
a radical improvement in rocketry was impossible.

00:00:34.746 --> 00:00:39.946
Space agencies and private companies tried
to build reusable rockets but failed.

00:00:39.946 --> 00:00:44.736
The only one that “kind of” worked was
NASA’s space shuttle - but the cost of refurbishing

00:00:44.736 --> 00:00:49.916
the boosters by fishing them out of the ocean
was so great that it didn’t make sense.

00:00:49.916 --> 00:00:52.806
You look at what others are doing.

00:00:52.806 --> 00:00:55.646
And say, ‘Well, others didn’t make it
work, so therefore it doesn’t work.’

00:00:55.646 --> 00:00:56.646
That has no basis in reality.

00:00:56.646 --> 00:01:05.206
A first principles foundational approach would
be to say, ‘Let’s look at the physics

00:01:05.206 --> 00:01:14.965
and economics of a rocket,’ and say, ‘If
we can analyze the physics of the rocket,

00:01:14.965 --> 00:01:17.136
is it physically possible?’

00:01:17.136 --> 00:01:21.895
First principles thinking would break down
the cost of the rocket’s materials and figure

00:01:21.895 --> 00:01:27.626
out how to reassemble the materials to produce
a more affordable rocket…which is SpaceX’s

00:01:27.626 --> 00:01:28.626
mission.

00:01:28.626 --> 00:01:34.426
Ad Astra school originally began out of SpaceX’s
rocket facility in Hawthorne, California,

00:01:34.426 --> 00:01:37.446
to teach Elon’s own children and the kids
of some employees.

00:01:37.446 --> 00:01:42.186
Then it moved to his house for a couple of
years, the one that formerly belonged to Gene

00:01:42.186 --> 00:01:43.186
Wilder.

00:01:43.186 --> 00:01:45.246
Elon was a guest lecturer.

00:01:45.246 --> 00:01:48.496
The teacher who helped create the curriculum
was Josh Dahn.

00:01:48.496 --> 00:01:49.496
Hi Josh

00:01:49.496 --> 00:01:50.496
Hi Cindy

00:01:50.496 --> 00:01:55.206
Dahn taught one of Elon’s sons at a private
school for gifted children in LA.

00:01:55.206 --> 00:02:01.115
The SpaceX CEO then approached him to start
an experimental school at his rocket factory.

00:02:01.115 --> 00:02:05.376
If Elon’s directive was to make the school
great.

00:02:05.376 --> 00:02:06.996
Okay, what does great look like?

00:02:06.996 --> 00:02:11.736
It looked like a totally different experience
than a typical school.

00:02:11.736 --> 00:02:17.816
Instead of classes in languages, music, or
sports, kids focused on critical thinking,

00:02:17.816 --> 00:02:22.236
solving the types of problems SpaceX employees
tackle.

00:02:22.236 --> 00:02:25.046
Imagine how the grid fin can be redesigned.

00:02:25.046 --> 00:02:28.975
He’s referring to the fins that come down
when the Falcon 9 lands.

00:02:28.975 --> 00:02:33.966
Could you imagine other designs and how would
you know if those designs would be more efficacious

00:02:33.966 --> 00:02:36.236
than the designs that are currently being
used.

00:02:36.236 --> 00:02:41.396
The lesson of first principles is to not be
bound by what’s already been done.

00:02:41.396 --> 00:02:46.286
You don’t have to create grid fins of the
same design, size, or material.

00:02:46.286 --> 00:02:51.406
You can even go back to the drawing board
completely and imagine something totally different

00:02:51.406 --> 00:02:53.966
that could direct a descending rocket.

00:02:53.966 --> 00:02:59.695
Can something other than grid fins be better
for the successor to the Falcon 9, Starship?

00:02:59.695 --> 00:03:04.377
There were also questions related to Tesla,
as students had exposure to Tesla’s design

00:03:04.377 --> 00:03:08.786
studio within SpaceX’s Hawthorne factory.

00:03:08.786 --> 00:03:12.976
What could the supercharger network in a country
where it doesn’t yet exist?

00:03:12.976 --> 00:03:15.566
What if you created a supercharger network
in Argentina?

00:03:15.566 --> 00:03:18.496
There are many different ways to answer this
question.

00:03:18.496 --> 00:03:22.055
What Josh was looking for was the ability
to reason.

00:03:22.055 --> 00:03:25.805
Centering superchargers around dense populations
is a start.

00:03:25.805 --> 00:03:28.305
And then the question is, “Where do we go
next?”

00:03:28.305 --> 00:03:32.586
And the way that they reason through that
is really interesting because then they’re

00:03:32.586 --> 00:03:35.466
seeing the multiple pathways they can approach.

00:03:35.466 --> 00:03:38.445
And one of the ways you can help them is to
limit the number of superchargers they can

00:03:38.445 --> 00:03:39.445
build.

00:03:39.445 --> 00:03:44.516
So if you can only build 50 superchargers
in the country of Argentina for that first

00:03:44.516 --> 00:03:46.406
year, where would those 50 be?

00:03:46.406 --> 00:03:48.725
This is some high-level thinking at a young
age.

00:03:48.725 --> 00:03:53.085
It’s remarkable what kids are capable of
when you give them opportunities where they

00:03:53.085 --> 00:03:54.966
have decisions to make.

00:03:54.966 --> 00:04:03.236
Josh now runs Synthesis, an online program
applying the same type of thinking at Elon’s

00:04:03.236 --> 00:04:05.966
school.

00:04:05.966 --> 00:04:10.766
Students from around the world strategize
in teams as a way to mirror collaboration

00:04:10.766 --> 00:04:11.766
in real life.

00:04:11.766 --> 00:04:14.406
We’ll start the first game in about five
minutes.

00:04:14.406 --> 00:04:19.316
Although the students are as young as eight,
the problems they have to answer are difficult

00:04:19.316 --> 00:04:20.756
even for adults.

00:04:20.756 --> 00:04:25.816
For example: Imagine that rocket travel is
as normal as plane travel.

00:04:25.816 --> 00:04:29.496
If you had to choose one future, which would
it be:

00:04:29.496 --> 00:04:32.366
Traveling to anywhere on Earth within an hour

00:04:32.366 --> 00:04:34.655
Going sightseeing on the moon

00:04:34.655 --> 00:04:37.336
Or 3), starting a city on Mars

00:04:37.336 --> 00:04:40.556
Again, there’s no right or wrong answer.

00:04:40.556 --> 00:04:46.176
But the idea is to start wondering which will
come first, and which one will change human

00:04:46.176 --> 00:04:48.066
life on earth most?

00:04:48.066 --> 00:04:51.656
It’s about figuring out what the future
will look like.

00:04:51.656 --> 00:04:53.956
And then, building that future.

00:04:53.956 --> 00:04:59.466
In the way that Tesla is looking to disrupt
transportation, and SpaceX the rocket industry,

00:04:59.466 --> 00:05:03.516
Elon is reimagining the future of education.

00:05:03.516 --> 00:05:08.016
Although his original private school is no
longer running, he’s opened up another Ad

00:05:08.016 --> 00:05:13.426
Astra school at SpaceX’s Starbase in Texas,
with a few dozen students.

00:05:13.426 --> 00:05:18.066
Will this type of education produce the next,
great entrepreneur?

00:05:18.066 --> 00:05:23.036
There are many ways to learn that can be more
effective than sitting in a classroom.

